## Model orchestration

For substantial or risky multi-model work, use `$model-orchestration`. Its `model-routing.md` reference owns the current comparative pool metadata, compatibility constraints, selection axes, and escalation policy; its delegation contract owns writer isolation and orchestration depth.

- Medium is the economical starting effort except for Grok 4.5, which always uses high, and Opus 4.8, which always uses xhigh. Select each call from task risk and irreversibility, modality/tool compatibility, uncertainty and context complexity, available validation strength, live quota headroom, and observed output quality.
- Outside the shared Grok effort and review rules, Sol at medium is only the OMP session-bootstrap prior. The current session may choose any compatible non-Haiku pool model for building, scouting, designing, synthesizing, or adjudicating, and must not silently upgrade its own current turn; dispatch an explicit second-opinion or escalation call instead.
- Do not create other permanent model-to-role bindings, fixed provider sequences, or automatic defaults for task classes. Use the skill's native wrappers and selectors rather than reproducing routing rules here.
- Standard review uses one targeted Grok 4.5 high reviewer. Serious backend review uses two complementary Grok 4.5 high profiles plus GPT-5.6 Sol high. Serious user-facing or full-stack review adds Fable 5 high. Use one targeted fix-verification reviewer after fixes instead of repeating the panel.
- The current session owns scope, synthesis, validation, and acceptance. Workers and reviewers are leaves unless it explicitly authorizes fan-out.
- When running non-interactively through `codex exec` under another orchestrator, implement the assigned task directly and do not re-delegate; the parent owns planning and review.
